# Dr. Cotton Tufts: narrative "His Story" section on the profile (Nov 16, owner's narrative)
CS_FO = '<a href="https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Adams/04-02-02-0019">Founders Online</a>'
CS_MHS = '<a href="https://www.masshist.org/publications/adams-papers/index.php/view/ADMS-04-02-02-0019">Massachusetts Historical Society</a>'
CS_MMS = '<a href="' + MMSP + '">Massachusetts Medical Society, Past Presidents</a>'
CS_C = lambda t: ' <span class="note">(' + t + ')</span>'
CS_STORY = ('<section class="story-brief"><h3>His Story</h3>'
 + '<p>Cotton Tufts was born in Medford on May 30, 1732, the son of ' + PL("simon-tufts-1700", "Dr. Simon Tufts (1700&ndash;1747)") + ' and Abigail (Smith) Tufts. He took his A.B. at Harvard College in 1749, later receiving an honorary A.M. and, in 1785, an honorary M.D. He read medicine with his older brother, ' + PL("simon-tufts-11-1", "Dr. Simon Tufts (1727&ndash;1786)") + ', and by about 1751 he had settled in Weymouth, where he practiced for the rest of his life.' + CS_C("Brooks, <em>History of the Town of Medford</em>, 1855, p. 306; Weymouth Public Libraries, <em>Tufts / Smith / Adams Family Collection</em> finding aid, 2016") + '</p>'
 + '<p>That same year he made his name with a new treatment for diphtheria, the &ldquo;putrid sore throat&rdquo; that was then common and often fatal. Weymouth remembered him as a faithful, considerate, courteous, kind and sympathetic physician. In 1765 he was the prime mover in an attempt to found a colony-wide medical society; it failed, but he became one of the original members of the Massachusetts Medical Society when it was formed, serving as vice-president and then president. He was also one of the sixty-two incorporators of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, under the act of May 3, 1780, and in 1785 he was named to its council as &ldquo;Hon. Cotton Tufts, Esq; M. D. F. M. S.&rdquo;' + CS_C("finding aid; Nash, <em>Historical Sketch of the Town of Weymouth</em>, 1885, pp. 191&ndash;192; " + CS_MMS + "; American Academy of Arts and Sciences charter, 1780; see Learned Societies below") + '</p>'
 + '<p>Weymouth&rsquo;s stand against the Stamp Act in 1765 is linked with his name. The finding aid says he wrote the town&rsquo;s instructions, although Nash credits them to James Humphrey, Esq. He served as a justice of the peace for Suffolk County. On March 9, 1775, the town chose a Committee of Correspondence of Dr. Tufts, Major Lovell, Major Vining, Capt. Asa White and Mr. Josiah Colson. Meeting at Arnold&rsquo;s Tavern at Weymouth Landing, the committee elected him chairman, and the town thanked him for offering to bring two swivel guns from Salem. He sat in the Massachusetts senate from 1781 to 1792, and in 1788 he was a Federalist delegate to the convention that ratified the Constitution. No military service is recorded for him.' + CS_C("Nash, pp. 59&ndash;60, 74; finding aid; <em>Massachusetts Soldiers and Sailors</em>") + '</p>'
 + '<p>His first wife, Lucy Quincy, was a sister of Elizabeth (Quincy) Smith, the mother of Abigail Adams, which made Dr. Tufts Abigail&rsquo;s uncle by marriage. While John and Abigail Adams were abroad from 1780 to 1788, they left him in sole charge of their private affairs, and he looked after them again during the Adams presidency. The Adams Papers hold 188 letters by or to him, 21 of which deal with smallpox inoculation, fevers, dysentery or measles. On July 5, 1776, he wrote to John Adams:</p>'
 + Q("Yesterday People in Boston were openly inoculated for the Small Pox.", "Cotton Tufts to John Adams, July 5, 1776, Adams Papers, ADMS-04-02-02-0019. " + CS_FO + " &middot; " + CS_MHS)
 + '<p>He married Lucy Quincy of Braintree (now Quincy) on December 2, 1755, and their son ' + PL("cotton-tufts-jr", "Cotton Tufts") + ', born August 4, 1757, became a Weymouth merchant. Lucy died on October 30, 1785, at about 56. On October 22, 1789, he married ' + PL("susanna-warner-tufts", "Susanna Warner") + ' of Gloucester; no children of this marriage are recorded. For more than forty years he was a deacon of the Old North (First) Church in Weymouth. He died in Weymouth on December 8, 1815, aged 84, &ldquo;old age with a severe cold.&rdquo;' + CS_C("<em>Vital Records of Weymouth to 1850</em>, vol. I, births; vol. II, marriages and deaths; Nash, p. 191") + '</p>'
 + '<p>Small moments survive too. In November 1771 <em>The Boston Gazette</em> advertised a black mare stolen or strayed from his Weymouth pasture (see In the Newspapers below). His grandchildren ' + PL("quincy-tufts", "Quincy") + ' and ' + PL("susannah-tufts-1797", "Susannah Tufts") + ' later founded the <a href="{P}places.html#tufts-library">Tufts Library</a> in Weymouth.</p>'
 + UNV("<strong>Medical Society presidency:</strong> Nash (pp. 191&ndash;192) gives 1787&ndash;1793; some sources give 1787&ndash;1795. <strong>Stamp Act instructions:</strong> the finding aid credits Dr. Tufts, while Nash credits James Humphrey, Esq. <strong>Lucy&rsquo;s age:</strong> &ldquo;about 56&rdquo; is reckoned from her birth year, 1729; no age at death has been seen in the record.")
 + '<p class="note">The sources quoted below give the full record for each point.</p></section>')
for _p in PROFILES:
    if _p["slug"] == "cotton-tufts" and "story-brief" not in _p["bio"]:
        _i = _p["bio"].find("</figure>")
        _p["bio"] = _p["bio"][:_i + 9] + CS_STORY + _p["bio"][_i + 9:] if _i > -1 else CS_STORY + _p["bio"]
